WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.980
THE STATE OF OPENAI:
OpenAI has devolved from being an altruistic

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.759
non profit to being a big tech cesspool.

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:29.460
Virtually everyone from Microsoft and Google
to Apple and Samsung want to create their

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.539
own version of ChatGPT or even buy out ChatGPT
itself.

00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:39.440
This really isn’t that surprising given
the roaring launch of ChatGPT.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.250
Despite being out for just a few months, ChatGPT
is already pulling in as much search interest

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:49.640
as Google Drive and 6 times more search interest
than Siri.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:55.910
But, while these numbers are impressive, they’re
also quite worrisome especially when you consider

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.530
the waning morals and ethics behind this technology.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.910
You see, it’s not just big tech that been
tainting the wholesome ambitions of OpenAI.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:09.659
OpenAI themselves, have slowly trended in
the direction of profit and corporate greed

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:11.729
as opposed to research and innovation.

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:17.340
In fact, this is one of the main reasons that
Elon Musk, one of the firm’s founders and

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.930
earliest advocates quit the company 5 years
ago actually.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:25.350
His parting message was that he didn’t agree
with what the firm wanted to do.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.320
More recently, he has doubled down on this
stance.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.220
He says that the entire vision of OpenAI was
to create a nonprofit opensource competitor

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.110
to Google, hence the name, OpenAI.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:44.689
But now, OpenAI has become a closed source,
maximum-profit company that’s effectively

00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:45.810
controlled by Microsoft.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.860
Despite this, the general public has embraced
ChatGPT with open arms.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:56.149
It’s ability to write school essays and
Google level code has seemingly overshadowed

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.360
it’s moral and ethical shortcomings.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:04.140
At this rate, it’s just a matter of time
until AI based search becomes a necessity

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:10.161
as opposed to a luxury which will simply give
big tech companies and new AI superstars even

00:02:10.161 --> 00:02:14.050
more control over our daily lives and society
at large.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:19.200
This becomes even more sketchy when you consider
that certain companies, Neuralink and Elon

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.160
Musk, want to put AI powered chips directly
into our brains.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:29.190
So, here’s the story of how OpenAI lost
their moral compass in record time and it’s

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:33.200
frightening implications for the future of
AI.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.260
ALTRUISTIC BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back, OpenAI didn’t just start

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:43.769
with good intentions, they started with altruistic
intentions.

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:48.720
Back in the mid 2010s, AI was an open frontier
with little competition.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.780
The best AI had to offer was voice assistants
that struggled to answer even the most basic

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:54.780
questions.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.480
But, a couple of influential people wanted
to change this.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:04.240
They wanted to create a new platform that
was focused on AI research and development.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:10.350
And unlike the existing options, they wanted
this platform to not be motivated by money.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:15.909
Ironically, all of the people behind this
effort were already extraordinarily rich.

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:19.540
This included the likes of Elon Musk, Peter
Thiel, and Reid Hoffman.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:24.611
There were also a few institutions involved
including Amazon Web Services, Infosys, and

00:03:24.611 --> 00:03:26.349
Y Combinator Research.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:32.360
But, at the same time, these people and entities
being rich was kind of a good thing.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:37.610
For one, given their extreme wealth, you would
think that they wouldn’t need more, so they

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.610
could stick to keeping OpenAI non-profit.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:44.490
And two, they could contribute large sums
of money to actually make this endeavor well

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.670
funded and viable.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:51.000
Looking back, we can confirm that they did
atleast stick to the second point.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.159
The company itself was founded with $1 billion
worth of funding.

00:03:55.159 --> 00:04:00.140
And according to OpenAI’s own blog, they
only expected spend a tiny fraction of this

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.319
over the next few years.

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:06.610
But, let’s just say that it didn’t take
long for things to get out of hand.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:11.219
Since then, OpenAI has managed to raise a
total of $11 billion.

00:04:11.219 --> 00:04:16.889
For perspective, OpenAI’s funding alone
is nearly worth the entire market cap of Domino’s

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:17.889
Pizza.

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:22.509
One of the first problems that the OpenAI
team faced was actually attracting talent.

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:26.720
For decades, Elon had relied on recruiting
truly passionate engineers to drive forward

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.020
Tesla and SpaceX.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.810
These engineers joined his companies out of
passion as opposed to the money, but Elon

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.590
couldn’t just use these same tactics with
OpenAI.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:42.340
Here’s the thing, when it comes to rockets
and space, engineers have a very limited number

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.590
of choices.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:50.060
They can either join Nasa, defense contracters,
or SpaceX, so convincing them to join SpaceX

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:51.710
wasn’t exactly difficult.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:57.139
But, with AI, engineers had the option to
join basically any big tech company that they

00:04:57.139 --> 00:04:58.139
wanted.

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:03.030
So, they weren’t exactly jumping up and
down with excitement trying to join a non

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.030
profit.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.389
It was actually quite the opposite.

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:12.020
Sure, OpenAI might’ve had nice intentions,
but they were by no means the only ones with

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.310
cool projects.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:18.220
So, there was no need for these highly talented
engineers to settle for a substantially lower

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.260
salary just to work at OpenAI.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:27.300
If anything, OpenAI had to outpay Google,
Amazon, and Apple to get engineers to ditch

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.410
the safety and stability of these companies.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.360
And this was much easier said than done.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.810
The primary reason that these companies are
able to pay so much is because much of their

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.590
compensation packages are derived from stock.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:45.889
But, OpenAI’s stock, well that was useless,
they were a nonprofit.

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:51.650
So, OpenAI had to pull a Netflix and offer
all cash compensations, but this depleted

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:54.050
the funding quite rapidly.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:59.400
You see, we’re not talking about salaries
of $200,000 or even $300,000.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:05.570
We’re talking about a median compensation
of $900,000 for software engineers.

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:12.039
At that rate, just 100 engineers would burn
$90 million every single year.

00:06:12.039 --> 00:06:17.669
OpenAI was quickly becoming a full on charity
as they lost money hand over first, and it

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:22.190
was just matter of time until they had to
do something.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:26.740
BECOMING FOR PROFIT:
It wasn’t just payroll that was weighing

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.740
down OpenAI either.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:34.340
It turns out that AI research in general is
just extraordinarily expensive.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:39.139
OpenAI would figure this out first hand as
they dove head first into AI development.

00:06:39.139 --> 00:06:44.530
Their first project was called OpenAI Gym
and this was a relatively tame endeavor.

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:48.960
Essentially, they had created a playground
for AI enthusiasts around to world to compete

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:49.960
on.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:53.910
These enthusiasts had to develop AI algorithms
that could beat Atari games, board games,

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.770
and other 2d and 3d simulations.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:07:01.160
And these enthusiasts would be ranked on a
leaderboard based on how versatile and robust

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.030
their algorithms were.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:07.650
Essentially, OpenAI was leveraging the competitive
side of engineers to research and develop

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.190
AI for free.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:13.930
Their second project, Universe, very much
relied on the same idea.

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:18.550
Instead of developing the AI themselves, OpenAI
opted to develop a platform that could let

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:23.240
anyone, or atleast anyone with advanced coding
knowledge, to test algorithms on the most

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:28.880
popular games and websites, but they couldn’t
rely on external sources forever.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:34.360
Eventually, they had to start developing their
own AI, but this was accompanied by insane

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.520
expenses almost instantaneously.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:40.030
Here’s the thing, how do you train AI?

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:45.810
Well, the answer is by processing a ridiculuous
amount of possibilites and outcomes which

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.430
requires a ludicrous amount of infrastructure.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:52.229
Take OpenAI’s Dota 2 bot for example.

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:57.690
To train this bot, OpenAI had to let the AI
play against itself for a simulated 10,000

00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:58.690
years.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:06.639
To simulate this many years, OpenAI had to
use 256 Nvidia GPUs and 128,000 CPU cores

00:08:06.639 --> 00:08:09.430
which they ironically rented from Google.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:16.150
But, even all of that was only able to simulate
a 180 years worth of gameplay every day meaning

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:20.680
that they had to run this setup for 56 days
just to complete initial training of the bot.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:26.050
At this rate, I don’t think you’d be surprised
to hear that OpenAI’s cloud computing expenses

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:30.680
alone would end up being $7.9 million in 2017.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:36.409
It was becoming abundantly clear that this
whole nonprofit benefit humanity thing just

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:37.899
wasn't gonna work.

00:08:37.899 --> 00:08:43.380
Company insiders were starting to propose
the idea of making OpenAI a for-profit endeavor.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:48.140
Being for profit and advancing humanity weren’t
mutually exclusive right?

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:51.740
They could still stick to their altruistic
intentions but leverage all of the financial

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:55.520
benefits that come from the world of busines
and especially venture capital.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:00.430
While this sounded good in theory, Elon Musk
knew that this would never play out in the

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:02.060
way that the team was hoping.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:07.200
The second that profit incentive was introduced,
it was just a matter of time until the company

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.410
became a soulless organization.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:14.940
Elon already had plenty of for profit AI endeavors
in his portfolio.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.610
OpenAI was designed to be something different.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:23.279
But, now that it too was becoming the same
thing, Elon decided that it was time to step

00:09:23.279 --> 00:09:24.279
away.

00:09:24.279 --> 00:09:28.209
Atleast, now, he wouldn’t have to compete
against himself for AI talent.

00:09:28.209 --> 00:09:32.779
Shortly after his departure, OpenAI would
complete the transition to becoming a for

00:09:32.779 --> 00:09:34.209
profit company.

00:09:34.209 --> 00:09:40.730
BIG TECH INVOLVEMENT:
Technically, OpenAI isn’t a full on for

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:41.920
profit company.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:49.740
It’s actually a capped for profit company,
but this doesn’t really mean much.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:54.380
Capped profit simply means that no investor
is allowed to profit more than 100X which

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:57.320
is an insanely generous limit.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:02.610
Even if you had invested in Apple right when
the iPhone was announced back in 2007, you’d

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.730
only have been up 40X even at the peak.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:10.760
So, it’s safe to say that investors won’t
have to worry about OpenAI’s profit cap

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:12.380
anytime soon.

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:18.220
Almost immediately after OpenAI transitioned
to being for profit, Microsoft got involved.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:25.079
Microsoft initially invested $1 billion into
OpenAI in mid 2019, and unsurprisingly, OpenAI

00:10:25.079 --> 00:10:28.959
would announce their new commercial intentions
in the same press release.

00:10:28.959 --> 00:10:34.020
But, if they wanted to pursue the commercial
avenue, they first needed a commercially viable

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:39.079
product leading them to revisit one of their
old research papers: “Improving Language

00:10:39.079 --> 00:10:43.060
Understanding by Generative Pre-Training”
or GPT.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:47.730
Initially, this was just a relatively small
project that was fueled by the contents of

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.480
7000 books.

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:56.170
But over time, they had improved the system
to rely on 8 million websites with 1.5 billion

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:57.230
parameters.

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:02.209
This was actually years ago, but the reason
that you probably didn’t know about this

00:11:02.209 --> 00:11:06.089
was because OpenAI decided to not release
GPT to the public.

00:11:06.089 --> 00:11:10.779
They felt that the system could easily be
used for nefarious purposes like writing scam

00:11:10.779 --> 00:11:12.940
emails and generating fake news.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:18.100
But, this worries quickly evaporated away
once Microsoft got involved.

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:23.810
From Microsoft’s perspective, this was their
big shot at taking on Google, and they weren’t

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:26.709
gonna let a couple of fake emails get in their
way.

00:11:26.709 --> 00:11:32.209
So, they gave the greenlight for developing
a commercially viable version of GPT and gave

00:11:32.209 --> 00:11:35.690
OpenAI resources that they could previously
only dream of.

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:43.519
They gave them access to a super computer
with 285,000 cpu cores and 10,000 gpus.

00:11:43.519 --> 00:11:47.810
This was the fifth most powerful computer
in the entire world.

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:51.100
You can’t just get access to these computers
by paying.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:56.350
To get access to such computers, you need
these megacorporations to be on your side,

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.959
and Microsoft was square on OpenAI’s side.

00:11:59.959 --> 00:12:05.910
OpenAI was able to use this computer to process
45TB of text data which translated to a whopping

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:09.220
175 billion parameters.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:14.870
OpenAI would go ahead and develop a chatbox
that could communicate with this GPT-3 AI

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:18.420
and this led to the creation of ChatGPT as
we know it today.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:22.910
But, that’s nothing in comparison to what
OpenAI is working on right now.

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:27.149
You know how GPT-3 was based on 175 billion
parameters?

00:12:27.149 --> 00:12:33.199
Well, GPT-4 is speculated to based on 100
trillion parameters and it may be launched

00:12:33.199 --> 00:12:35.540
by the end of this year.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:41.540
As you can see, OpenAI is just getting started,
but big tech already has a tight grip on the

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:46.830
company, and you can be sure that they will
monetize anything and everything that this

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:49.320
company will ever be.

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:55.860
THE FUTURE OF OPENAI:
In the end, it’s easy to see why OpenAI

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:57.791
turned to the darkside.

00:12:57.791 --> 00:13:02.579
They tried to go the opensource route and
advance AI research and development, but the

00:13:02.579 --> 00:13:07.480
reality is that even with the richest people
in the world can’t fund this project.

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:11.959
This vision can only be funded by a corporate
level wealth in the hundreds billions.

00:13:11.959 --> 00:13:17.250
But, don’t be fooled though, these companies
aren’t investing hundreds of billions of

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:18.790
the goodness of their hearts.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:24.120
They’re well aware that the next frontier
is AI and machine learning, and they want

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:28.970
to maximize the chances that they’re the
winners of that frontier as well.

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:34.050
And while it may seem like we’re getting
all of these cool products for free, it’s

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:38.829
just a matter of time until there’s a catch
whether that be simple data collection or

00:13:38.829 --> 00:13:41.589
control over our entire lives.

00:13:41.589 --> 00:13:46.800
Either way though, it’s just a matter of
time until we see OpenAI become the villain,

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:48.860
but that’s just what I think.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:52.020
Are you excited or scared for the future of
AI?

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:53.610
Comment that down below.

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:58.220
Also, drop a like if you wish that the original
OpenAI had come to fruition.

